Subject: Re: BridgeKit quick-handoff: SDK quickstart, auth, routing, and local tests

Alex — quick answers and a couple of concrete artifacts attached. Pasted the most relevant bits inline for convenience.

1) mTLS + rotation: Yes — BridgeKit supports mTLS with client certs issued by your internal CA. We use a rolling reload (zero-downtime hot-reload) for certs in the runtime; the sample includes a small script (cert_rotate.sh) that demonstrates rotation against a local nginx terminator used in the Dockerized quickstart.

2) Embeddings/reranker: the samples use 1024-dim embeddings (the repo has a small fixture ./data/sample_embeddings_1024.npy) and we show a local FAISS index (Flat + IVF example). For production you can plug whatever ANN index you want; the reranker sample demonstrates two-pass: quick ANN nearest neighbors (k=50) then an explicit model score pass over top-5 candidates.

3) Windows port conflict: the local_run.sh binds to 127.0.0.1:8080 by default. Set PORT env var (e.g., set PORT=0 to let OS pick a free port in the TS sample) or run node ./ts/server.js --port 0. I've uploaded a tiny patch ts-auth-example.patch that adds a --port flag and better Windows compatibility.

4) TS reranker snippet (paste-ready):

// ts/bridgekit-rerank.ts (excerpt)
import BridgeKit from 'bridgekit-sdk';
const client = new BridgeKit({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });

async function rerank(query, candidates) {
  // embeddings call (first pass)
  const qVec = await client.embeddings.create({ model: 'rbw-embed-v1', input: query });
  const hits = localFaiss.search(qVec, 50); // returns top-50 ids

  // fetch candidate texts and then score with the model (second pass)
  const scores = await Promise.all(hits.slice(0,5).map(async (id) => {
    const doc = await loadDocById(id);
    const res = await client.completions.create({
      model: 'rbw-reranker-v1',
      input: `Score: ${query}

Candidate: ${doc.text}

Return a numeric score 0-100`,
      max_tokens: 6
    });
    return { id, score: Number(res.output_text.trim()) };
  }));
  return scores.sort((a,b) => b.score - a.score);
}

I attached: ts-auth-example.patch (adds port flag & env-var fallback), local-smoke-test-checklist.md (detailed steps), and a small FAISS fixture.

If you want, we can also add a small Makefile to orchestrate a FAISS build on Windows WSL if your team prefers. Happy to hop on a quick screen-share to step through the patch.

